{{also|ōhō}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|o ho}}; {{m|enm|o, ho}}; equivalent to {{m|en|o}} + {{m|en|ho}}.

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{n-g|Expressing surprise or gloating realisation}}; [[aha]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=Lucy Bethia Walford|title=Troublesome Daughters|passage="There is Kate, taking no heed of anybody; sensible old darling &mdash; she goes at her tea and cake &mdash; '''Oho'''! she has not touched them!"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=Rupert Hughes|title=What Will People Say?|passage="'''Oho''', my boy, that's the woman who keeps you here! Mrs. Neff hinted at it, but I wouldn't believe it till I had it from you."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Thomas Flanagan|title=The Tenants of Time|passage="'''Oho,'''" he said, "a vile thing to say. It is. I am losing check upon my tongue, it is running free like a riderless horse. And I don't give a damn. I can say at last what I wanted for years to say, years of being politic and demure. No longer."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Bruce A Shuman|title=Beyond the library of the future|passage="'''Oho'''! Now I see where he's going with this, Frank thinks. Would have seen it earlier if I hadn't been so tired."}}

